  "details": "With 2026.4.2, public instances now assign their creator as the instance owner, giving them moderation powers. I agree public instances need more moderation, but this hands those powers to whoever clicked “create instance,” while world creators and their moderation teams still can’t properly protect the worlds they built. Meanwhile, groups have more authority in our worlds than we do.\n\nAs a creator of two popular worlds with a moderation team, here’s what we run into:\n\n- Group instances run by problematic users, or groups that have banned us, leave us unable to keep our own worlds safe.\n\n- Bans don’t stick. An instance ban ends when the instance does, and even a direct kick only lasts 60 minutes before the same people come right back.\n\n- Instance owners and world authors are now on the same moderation tier, so a random instance owner can kick the author or their moderators, just like group owners already can.\n\n- The TOS allows creator-made moderation tools but prohibits blacklists and permanent bans, so those tools can’t keep repeat offenders out. Creators have been reprimanded for trying.\n\nWhat I’m proposing:\n\n- Persistent world-level bans set by the world author, applied to Public and Group Public instances of that world.\n\n- Author-appointed moderators with moderation powers in every Public and Group Public instance of that world, assigned through the creator’s group or in Unity.\n\n- A moderation tier above instance owners and group moderators for world authors and their appointed mods, so groups can’t kick or abuse their powers against the people making sure rules are enforced and the instance is safe.\n\n- Udon access to these bans and moderator roles, so creators can build on them without needing external blacklists.\n\nTo prevent abuse, bans would only apply to Public and Group Public instances. Private, Friends, and Invite instances would stay accessible, so no one is locked out of a world entirely. Banned users could see that they’re banned and appeal to the world author, and VRChat could revoke these tools from creators who abuse them.\n\nSafety is still the number one issue in public instances, and this change is a step in the wrong direction. Creators should already have these tools, or at least be allowed to use their own without being reprimanded under the TOS. Most creators have good intentions, and the ones who run these spaces every day are the most willing to stop predators and bad actors from moving freely between instances. It’s gone on too long with creators having no real power while groups have more control over our worlds than we do. Something needs to change. Give us the tools to do it properly.",
